    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nA type declared with `ctypedef object MyObj` is an alias for the generic Python `object` type, but since Cython 3.1.0 it no longer behaves like one: assigning or returning a literal (`str`, `bytes`, or `unicode` constant) to a `ctypedef object` target fails to compile with `Cannot assign type '… object' to 'MyObj'`, whereas the identical code targeting plain `object` compiles. Only literals are affected — a variable of the same type assigns fine. It reproduces under every `language_level` (`3`, `3str`, `2`). This compiled on Cython 3.0.x.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\n# cython: language_level=3\n\nctypedef object MyObj          # alias for the generic Python `object`\n\ncdef object accept():\n    return b\"x\"                # OK\n\ncdef MyObj reject():\n    return b\"x\"                # ERROR: Cannot assign type 'bytes object' to 'MyObj'\n\n# Same failure with a str literal (any language_level: 3 / 3str / 2):\ncdef MyObj reject_str():\n    return \"x\"                 # ERROR: Cannot assign type 'str object' to 'MyObj'\n\n# But a variable of the same type is accepted — only *literals* fail:\ncdef MyObj ok_var():\n    cdef str s = \"x\"\n    return s                   # OK\n```\n\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n`ctypedef object MyObj` is an alias for `object`. Since a `str`/`bytes`/`unicode` value is an `object`, returning or assigning one — literal or not — to a `MyObj` target should be accepted, exactly as it is for plain `object` (and as it was in Cython ≤ 3.0.x).\n\n### OS\n\nWindows, Linux\n\n### Python version\n\n3.11, 3.14\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.1.0, 3.2.4\n\n### Additional context\n\nRegression range: fails on 3.1.0, 3.1.6, 3.2.4; works on 3.0.12 and earlier 3.0.x. Python-version- and platform-independent",
